{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>In the second chariot.</b> Second to his chariot — proceeding alongside his." "<b>Avrech.</b> As the Targum renders: ״דֵּין אַבָּא לְמַלְכָ֐א.״ רֵיךְ in Aramaic (some say: in Roman) means king. In the construct state there is no רֵיכָא and no בַּר רֵיכָא. And in aggadic interpretation, Rabbi Yehudah expounded: אַבְרֵךְ — this is Yosef, who is an אָב in wisdom and רַךְ in years. Rabbi Yosi ben Durmaskit said to him: How long will you distort the verses for us? אַבְרֵךְ is nothing but a term for knees — that all would come in and go out under his hand, as it says: וְנָתוֹן אוֹתוֹ וְגוֹ׳."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {1 "The phrase בְּהַשֻּׁתָּפִין לָא רֵיכָא וְלֹא בַּר רֵיכָא is a grammatical observation: in the construct/smiteḥut form, the Aramaic word רֵיכָא ('king') does not appear, nor does בַּר רֵיכָא ('son of a king'); the form אַבְרֵךְ therefore cannot derive from that root."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:03:05.748640338Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}